    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101">

                <pb facs="0181" n="174" sameAs="frus:frus1894Nicaragua/pg174"/>

                <head>No. 101.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Baker to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States,<lb/>Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1894-08-30"><hi rendition="#italic">August
                                30, 1894</hi></date>. (Received September 14,
                    1894.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I received your cable and I at once sent
                    the inclosed “demand” to the Nicaraguan Government.</p>
                <pb facs="0182" n="175" xml:id="pg175"/>
                <p>I may here remark that Messrs. Lampton and Wiltbank, the two Americans who were
                    brought to Managua, and afterwards sent out of the country without trial, were
                    not aware that the United States naval commander had made any protest against
                    their arbitrary arrest, treatment, and expulsion. The commander sent no word of
                    information to me, and I was left with the inference that our commander knew
                    that these men were guilty of fomenting trouble and should be sent out of the
                    country. This seeming action, or want of action, on the part of the commander
                    made it the more difficult for me to know how much I should demand.</p>
                <p>Besides, having in view the fact that I made a “demand” in the Argiiello case in
                    the name of the United States, which was virtually ignored by this Government
                    and apparently dropped by ours, I tried in this case to be especially judicious
                    and to not protest too vigorously.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lewis Baker.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d101-a1.xml" xpointer="d101-a1"/>
            </div>
